<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cynddelw Brydydd Mawr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1155–<a href="/years/1195">1195</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Cynddelw the Great Poet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cynddelw Brydydd Mawr ('the Great Poet') was one of the foremost Welsh court poets of the twelfth century. He was poet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madog-ap-maredudd">Madog ap Maredudd</a> of Powys, for whom he wrote praise poems and an elegy, and later composed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-gwynedd">Owain Gwynedd</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-gruffydd">the Lord Rhys</a> and other princes. Nearly fifty of his poems survive, more than for any other poet of the princes.</p></div>
